**What the packet rests on.** Thirteen verbatim ASAP Sports press transcripts from January 2023 through the 2026 US Open final, plus two long-form interviews (Highsnobiety, On), the 2022 ESPN pre-fame profile, father testimony from four relays, and rival testimony from Djokovic and Alcaraz. Current status is checked through 2026-09-20: he beat Alcaraz for the first time in a 3:33 a.m. quarterfinal on September 9, lost his first major final to Zverev on September 13, and is ranked No. 4. Those are logged as time-sensitive claims with review triggers so the article does not depend on them.

**The type argument.** Type 3 leads on a single discriminator from January 2023, said before any media polish: a supportive crowd creates "this expectation that you have to deliver" and the fear of "letting them down," while hostility lets him "play free." Evaluation is the burden he names. That pairs with a four-year self-story of chasing the people ahead of him and a deliberate narrowing of his persona toward "focused" and "professional" once it produced results. Type 7 is the serious rival and explains the joy-in-pain register, the loss reframing, the plan-abandoning, and the refusal to be constrained by umpires, curfews or Zverev's rhythm. I flagged contested typing because the two readings are close and the press-conference source base tilts toward a 3 register.

